In Spring Framework AOP, there are two main ways aspects are applied:

Proxy-based AOP (Spring AOP)

AspectJ AOP

Understanding their difference is a very common backend interview question.

1️⃣ Proxy-based AOP (Spring AOP)

This is the default AOP used in Spring Boot applications.

Spring creates a proxy object around your bean.

How it works
Client
  ↓
Proxy Object
  ↓
Target Method

When the client calls the method:

Call goes to proxy

Proxy executes aspect logic

Then calls actual method

Example Flow
UserService (Original Bean)
      ↑
   Proxy Bean

Client calls:

userService.createUser();

Actually it calls:

Proxy → Aspect → Real Method
Important Limitation

Proxy-based AOP only works for public methods and external method calls.

Example problem:

public void methodA() {
    methodB();   // AOP will NOT work here
}

Because internal method calls bypass proxy.

2️⃣ AspectJ AOP

AspectJ works differently.
It modifies the actual bytecode of the class.

So aspects are woven directly into the class.

How it works
Client
  ↓
Modified Class (with aspect inside)

No proxy is needed.

Advantages

AspectJ can apply AOP to:

private methods

protected methods

constructors

field access

internal method calls

3️⃣ Visual Difference
Spring Proxy AOP
Client
  ↓
Proxy
  ↓
Target Class
AspectJ AOP
Client
  ↓
Aspect + Target (woven together)
4️⃣ Performance Difference
| Feature         | Spring Proxy AOP | AspectJ          |
| --------------- | ---------------- | ---------------- |
| Implementation  | Proxy            | Bytecode weaving |
| Internal calls  | ❌ Not supported  | ✅ Supported      |
| Private methods | ❌                | ✅                |
| Performance     | Slightly slower  | Faster           |
| Complexity      | Easy             | More complex     |

5️⃣ What Companies Usually Use

Most microservices built with Spring Boot use:

✅ Spring Proxy AOP

Because:

Easy to configure

Works well for logging, transactions, security

AspectJ is used only when deep bytecode-level AOP is required.

🔑 Interview Answer (Short)

Spring AOP is proxy-based and applies aspects by creating proxy objects around beans,
 while AspectJ performs bytecode weaving and can apply aspects directly to classes, including private methods and internal calls.